WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.991
Here is the constraint most regulated teams end up with.

2
00:00:06.991 --> 00:00:14.041
You may not let a model generate user interface at runtime, because every screen a customer sees has to be reviewed before it ships.

3
00:00:14.041 --> 00:00:18.421
So you go the template route: pre-authored, reviewed structures, filled with data.

4
00:00:18.421 --> 00:00:21.519
That works beautifully as long as the data is predictable.

5
00:00:21.519 --> 00:00:23.442
Then you hit a case where it is not.

6
00:00:23.442 --> 00:00:26.700
An account with no holdings, or one holding, or four hundred.

7
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.958
Groups nested two deep for one customer and flat for another.

8
00:00:29.958 --> 00:00:33.910
The instinct is that templates have run out and you now need generated UI.

9
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.688
That instinct is wrong, and the rest of this is why.

10
00:00:36.688 --> 00:00:40.900
The answer is already in the protocol, in a place that is easy to read past.

11
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.282
A component's children property accepts two different things.

12
00:00:44.282 --> 00:00:47.774
The obvious one is an array of child identifiers, a fixed list.

13
00:00:47.774 --> 00:00:51.322
The second one is an object: a component identifier, and a path.

14
00:00:51.322 --> 00:00:55.812
That says render this one component once for every item in the list at that path.

15
00:00:55.812 --> 00:01:00.247
So the number of rows on screen is a property of the data, not of the structure.

16
00:01:00.247 --> 00:01:05.846
You write one row, review one row, and the same reviewed structure renders zero rows or four hundred.

17
00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:09.754
Here is that in practice.

18
00:01:09.754 --> 00:01:15.188
A column whose children are a template pointing at slash holdings, and the row component it names.

19
00:01:15.188 --> 00:01:18.404
That row is two text components bound to symbol and value.

20
00:01:18.404 --> 00:01:20.678
Notice those paths have no leading slash.

21
00:01:20.678 --> 00:01:27.110
Inside a template, a bare path is relative to the item currently being rendered, so symbol means this item's symbol.

22
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:31.435
The column does not know how many rows there will be, and it does not need to.

23
00:01:31.435 --> 00:01:33.653
Nothing here changes when the data does.

24
00:01:33.653 --> 00:01:41.195
One more thing to notice: the row itself is a Row component with two children, so it is an ordinary reviewed component in every respect.

25
00:01:41.195 --> 00:01:43.856
There is nothing special about being a template.

26
00:01:43.856 --> 00:01:50.122
Any component can be one, and the same component can be a template in one place and a plain child somewhere else.

27
00:01:50.122 --> 00:01:53.122
That relative path is doing more work than it appears to.

28
00:01:53.122 --> 00:01:58.902
Because a path inside a template resolves against the current item, a template can contain another template.

29
00:01:58.902 --> 00:02:02.327
This is a group block: a card, a title bound to the group's name

30
00:02:02.327 --> 00:02:05.966
and then a column whose children are a template pointing at holdings

31
00:02:05.966 --> 00:02:07.143
with no leading slash.

32
00:02:07.143 --> 00:02:08.909
That means this group's holdings.

33
00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:12.709
And the component it renders is the same holding row from a moment ago.

34
00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:17.793
One reviewed row component, used at two different depths, against two different shapes of data.

35
00:02:17.793 --> 00:02:23.787
That is how a fixed component tree renders a structure whose depth and width you did not know when you wrote it.

36
00:02:23.787 --> 00:02:25.927
It is worth pausing on why this is safe.

37
00:02:25.927 --> 00:02:29.888
The nesting depth is bounded by the components you wrote, not by the data.

38
00:02:29.888 --> 00:02:32.724
A group can contain holdings because you said it can.

39
00:02:32.724 --> 00:02:39.467
If the data arrives three levels deep and your templates only nest two, you get two levels rendered, not an unexpected screen.

40
00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:44.016
The data can change how much you see; it can never invent a shape you did not author.

41
00:02:44.016 --> 00:02:48.619
And this is not theoretical for you, because your own brokerage payload already does it.

42
00:02:48.619 --> 00:02:50.973
Four template bindings, two of them relative.

43
00:02:50.973 --> 00:02:55.525
Groups render from a template, positions render from a template relative to their group

44
00:02:55.525 --> 00:03:00.703
and the key value row template is used twice at two different depths for two different data shapes.

45
00:03:00.703 --> 00:03:04.679
That payload renders three groups, each with a different number of positions

46
00:03:04.679 --> 00:03:08.864
each with a different number of detail rows, from a component tree written once.

47
00:03:08.864 --> 00:03:13.572
The pattern you need for non-deterministic data is the pattern already in your repository.

48
00:03:13.572 --> 00:03:17.652
Which is the argument to take to your reviewers, because it is not a proposal.

49
00:03:17.652 --> 00:03:23.354
It is a description of something already running in your codebase that they have presumably already approved.

50
00:03:24.256 --> 00:03:28.954
So let us push it. Same components, four datasets that look nothing like each other.

51
00:03:28.954 --> 00:03:33.996
This function is the only thing that differs between the four screens you are about to see.

52
00:03:33.996 --> 00:03:38.040
Empty. One holding. Forty holdings. And three groups of holdings, nested.

53
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:44.743
Different lengths, different shapes, one with a populated groups list and an empty flat list and one the other way round.

54
00:03:44.743 --> 00:03:47.956
The component tree above it is untouched between all four.

55
00:03:47.956 --> 00:03:54.603
And note what is not in this function: there is no component identifier, no layout, no branching on which widget to use.

56
00:03:54.603 --> 00:03:58.370
It returns values. That is the line you want to be able to point at.

57
00:04:00.874 --> 00:04:01.917
Start with nothing.

58
00:04:01.917 --> 00:04:06.858
The template at slash holdings produces no children, so the list renders as nothing at all

59
00:04:06.858 --> 00:04:13.830
and the empty message is a text component bound to a string that happens to have content in this dataset and not in the others.

60
00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:20.144
That is worth saying plainly: the empty state is not a different screen, it is the same screen with different data.

61
00:04:20.144 --> 00:04:22.303
Tap through and now there is one holding.

62
00:04:22.303 --> 00:04:25.780
The empty message went away because its bound string is now blank.

63
00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.204
One row appeared because the list at slash holdings has one item.

64
00:04:29.204 --> 00:04:31.311
No new components were sent to get here.

65
00:04:34.724 --> 00:04:39.673
Again, and now there are forty. Forty rows, from the same single row component, scrolling.

66
00:04:39.673 --> 00:04:46.877
If you were generating this, forty rows would be forty component definitions crossing the network and forty things nobody reviewed.

67
00:04:46.877 --> 00:04:50.232
Here it is one reviewed component and a list of forty values.

68
00:04:50.232 --> 00:04:52.981
This is also where the performance argument lands.

69
00:04:52.981 --> 00:04:57.381
Forty rows of generated components would be forty definitions parsed and diffed.

70
00:04:57.381 --> 00:05:03.815
Here the client already has the row and is mapping a list over it, which is the same work a native list adapter does.

71
00:05:03.815 --> 00:05:08.819
Bigger data does not mean a bigger payload of structure, it means a longer array of values.

72
00:05:08.819 --> 00:05:15.822
And the last one changes shape entirely: three groups, each holding a different number of positions, rendered by the nested template.

73
00:05:15.822 --> 00:05:19.298
Technology with six, financials with three, healthcare with three.

74
00:05:19.298 --> 00:05:24.089
Two levels deep, and still the same component tree that drew the empty screen a minute ago.

75
00:05:24.089 --> 00:05:26.196
Depth and width both came from the data.

76
00:05:26.196 --> 00:05:30.040
And if a fourth dataset arrived tomorrow with ten groups instead of three

77
00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.304
or three levels instead of two where your templates support it

78
00:05:33.304 --> 00:05:35.990
nothing here would need rebuilding or re-reviewing.

79
00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.359
That is the property you are actually buying.

80
00:05:38.359 --> 00:05:42.024
That is the visible half. The wire tells the other half of the story.

81
00:05:42.024 --> 00:05:44.665
Here is the agent's log for that whole sequence.

82
00:05:44.665 --> 00:05:50.056
One create surface and one update components at the start, and after that, only update data model.

83
00:05:50.056 --> 00:05:54.842
Three times. The structure crossed the network once, at the beginning, and never again.

84
00:05:54.842 --> 00:05:59.683
Every screen change after that was data. That has three consequences worth caring about.

85
00:05:59.683 --> 00:06:03.149
It is small, because you are sending values rather than a tree.

86
00:06:03.149 --> 00:06:08.045
It is fast, because the client is diffing a data model rather than rebuilding components.

87
00:06:08.045 --> 00:06:15.581
And it is auditable, because the structure a customer saw is a known artifact and the only variable is the data you can log alongside it.

88
00:06:15.581 --> 00:06:18.552
It also means a re-render is cheap enough to do often.

89
00:06:18.552 --> 00:06:23.338
If holdings update every few seconds, you are sending a small data model, not a screen.

90
00:06:23.338 --> 00:06:26.347
One template will not always be enough, and that is fine.

91
00:06:26.347 --> 00:06:29.883
The answer is not to start generating, it is to have more than one.

92
00:06:29.883 --> 00:06:33.691
Keep a dictionary of templates, every one of them written and reviewed.

93
00:06:33.691 --> 00:06:37.821
Then write a pure function that maps the shape of the data to one identifier.

94
00:06:37.821 --> 00:06:40.770
No holdings and no groups gives you the empty template.

95
00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:44.257
Groups present gives you the grouped one. Otherwise the flat one.

96
00:06:44.257 --> 00:06:46.402
Three things matter about that function.

97
00:06:46.402 --> 00:06:50.746
It is deterministic, so the same inputs give the same screen and you can test it.

98
00:06:50.746 --> 00:06:57.129
It asserts that the identifier exists, so there is no path where an unexpected shape falls back to something generated.

99
00:06:57.129 --> 00:07:03.994
And it logs the identifier alongside the surface, so what a customer saw is a commit hash plus a data model rather than a guess.

100
00:07:03.994 --> 00:07:07.962
The agent is choosing between reviewed options. It is never composing one.

101
00:07:07.962 --> 00:07:15.578
That distinction is the whole thing your reviewers care about, and it is worth writing down in exactly those words when you take this to them.

102
00:07:15.578 --> 00:07:18.582
One more detail worth copying: make that function total.

103
00:07:18.582 --> 00:07:23.302
Every shape of data should map to some template, including the shapes you did not expect

104
00:07:23.302 --> 00:07:26.520
because the alternative is an exception path at request time

105
00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:30.220
and exception paths are exactly where unreviewed behaviour creeps in.

106
00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:35.154
An explicit fallback template that says we could not render this is still a reviewed screen.

107
00:07:35.203 --> 00:07:39.634
There is one more move that saves you templates, and it is the one teams miss most often.

108
00:07:39.634 --> 00:07:44.764
Suppose two screens differ only in that one shows the amount large and one shows it normal.

109
00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:46.455
The instinct is two templates.

110
00:07:46.455 --> 00:07:52.091
But variant is a property like any other, so you can bind it to the data model instead of fixing it.

111
00:07:52.091 --> 00:07:56.093
The agent sends h2 or body in the data, and the structure is identical.

112
00:07:56.093 --> 00:08:04.154
That works for anything the catalog models as an enumerated property: text variants, button variants, row and column alignment, list direction.

113
00:08:04.154 --> 00:08:07.874
There is a limit, and it is the one we hit earlier in this series.

114
00:08:07.874 --> 00:08:10.692
The value has to be one the catalog already allows

115
00:08:10.692 --> 00:08:14.243
and an unknown value renders an error chip rather than throwing

116
00:08:14.243 --> 00:08:20.218
so you validate against the catalog schema before you send rather than finding out on a customer's screen.

117
00:08:20.218 --> 00:08:23.431
Which sharpens the test into three cases rather than two.

118
00:08:23.431 --> 00:08:26.193
Varies in how many: a template with a bound list.

119
00:08:26.193 --> 00:08:31.266
Varies in degree: a bound enumerated property. Varies in kind: a second reviewed template.

120
00:08:31.266 --> 00:08:35.324
Most of what teams call non-deterministic turns out to be the first two.

121
00:08:35.324 --> 00:08:40.917
One more property of this approach is worth having, because it solves a problem that usually gets solved badly.

122
00:08:40.917 --> 00:08:45.506
Because structure and data are separate messages, they do not have to arrive together.

123
00:08:45.506 --> 00:08:51.270
This function sends create surface and update components the first time, and after that only the data model.

124
00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:55.006
Which means you can put the skeleton on screen before the data exists.

125
00:08:55.006 --> 00:08:57.301
If your positions service takes two seconds

126
00:08:57.301 --> 00:09:00.343
send the structure immediately and the data when it lands

127
00:09:00.343 --> 00:09:03.438
and the customer sees a real screen rather than a spinner.

128
00:09:03.438 --> 00:09:06.267
And because the data model can be replaced repeatedly

129
00:09:06.267 --> 00:09:09.469
you can stream in stages: the summary as soon as you have it

130
00:09:09.469 --> 00:09:11.710
the holdings when the slower call returns.

131
00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:13.098
The client diffs each one.

132
00:09:13.098 --> 00:09:14.913
Nothing about the template changes

133
00:09:14.913 --> 00:09:18.648
and you never had to decide in advance what a loading state looks like

134
00:09:18.648 --> 00:09:22.171
because the loading state is just the screen with less data in it.

135
00:09:22.171 --> 00:09:26.654
And because the client keeps the structure, a reconnect does not mean re-sending it.

136
00:09:26.654 --> 00:09:29.696
You send the data model again and the screen is restored.

137
00:09:29.696 --> 00:09:32.827
Now the honest part, because this pattern is not universal.

138
00:09:32.827 --> 00:09:36.093
First, version zero point nine has no conditional rendering.

139
00:09:36.093 --> 00:09:39.360
You cannot say render this card only when the list is empty.

140
00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.463
You can bind text to an empty string so it takes no space

141
00:09:42.463 --> 00:09:45.620
or bind a list to nothing so its template produces nothing

142
00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:48.723
but a container bound to nothing still draws its own box.

143
00:09:48.723 --> 00:09:56.780
That is why the empty state here is a bare text rather than a card: I built it as a card first and it left an empty rectangle on every other screen.

144
00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:59.229
Second, variation in kind is still structure.

145
00:09:59.229 --> 00:10:05.653
A payment that needs a date picker and one that needs a dropdown are two templates, not one template with clever data.

146
00:10:05.653 --> 00:10:10.280
Third, if the set of possible screens is genuinely open ended, no fixed set covers it

147
00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:15.669
and that is the case where the constraint is doing its job by telling you the requirement is wrong.

148
00:10:15.669 --> 00:10:18.718
Which gives you a test you can apply in a design review.

149
00:10:18.718 --> 00:10:21.875
Ask whether the thing varies in how many, or in what kind.

150
00:10:21.875 --> 00:10:24.325
How many is data, and one template covers it.

151
00:10:24.325 --> 00:10:31.511
What kind is structure, and it needs another reviewed template, or the variation needs to move into a bound property like a variant.

152
00:10:31.511 --> 00:10:34.831
There is a fourth limit worth naming, which is version drift.

153
00:10:34.831 --> 00:10:41.201
These enumerations are catalog properties, so they can change between catalog versions independently of the protocol.

154
00:10:41.201 --> 00:10:48.985
Pin the catalog identifier you author against, and treat a catalog upgrade as a change that needs the same snapshot tests as a template change.

155
00:10:48.985 --> 00:10:55.714
And if the kind variation keeps multiplying templates, there is one more move, and it is the one your team is already positioned for.

156
00:10:55.714 --> 00:10:58.104
Stop adding templates and add a component.

157
00:10:58.104 --> 00:11:02.826
A custom component has a name, a property schema, and a composable content function

158
00:11:02.826 --> 00:11:07.889
and inside that content function you are writing ordinary Compose in your own repository.

159
00:11:07.889 --> 00:11:12.213
So an equity renders three lines, an option renders strike, expiry and delta

160
00:11:12.213 --> 00:11:17.277
and a bond renders coupon, maturity and yield, all from one component with one wire name.

161
00:11:17.277 --> 00:11:21.999
The agent just sends component position row with a kind property bound to the data.

162
00:11:21.999 --> 00:11:25.981
The reason this satisfies the constraint is worth being precise about.

163
00:11:25.981 --> 00:11:28.257
That branching ships inside your binary.

164
00:11:28.257 --> 00:11:34.060
It goes through code review, your test suite and release sign-off, exactly like any other screen code.

165
00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:38.497
It is not generated, it does not cross the wire, and an agent cannot alter it.

166
00:11:38.497 --> 00:11:46.235
What an agent can do is say render a position row with this data, which is the same kind of statement as render a text with this string.

167
00:11:46.235 --> 00:11:51.184
The cost is real: a custom component is a renderer change on every platform you support

168
00:11:51.184 --> 00:11:54.939
which for you means doing it on Android and on the web and on iOS.

169
00:11:54.939 --> 00:12:00.629
So it is worth it when one component collapses five templates, and it is not worth it for a one-off.

170
00:12:00.629 --> 00:12:05.123
But since your team is building custom renderers anyway, this is not extra work

171
00:12:05.123 --> 00:12:08.935
it is the thing you were already going to build, used deliberately.

172
00:12:08.935 --> 00:12:15.672
Before the governance piece, here is the test applied to real requirements, because that is where this becomes useful on a Monday.

173
00:12:15.672 --> 00:12:18.703
These are the kinds of lines that end up in a ticket.

174
00:12:18.703 --> 00:12:24.707
A positions list of anywhere from zero to four hundred rows: how many, so one template with a bound list.

175
00:12:24.707 --> 00:12:31.340
Show unrealised profit and loss in red when negative: that is degree, so bind a property and add no template at all.

176
00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:37.344
Retail customers see four summary rows and advisors see nine: still how many, same template, longer list.

177
00:12:37.344 --> 00:12:39.174
Options positions needing strike

178
00:12:39.174 --> 00:12:43.062
expiry and delta where equities do not: that is how many detail rows

179
00:12:43.062 --> 00:12:47.637
which is already a template, unless options genuinely need a different component

180
00:12:47.637 --> 00:12:49.066
and then it becomes kind.

181
00:12:49.066 --> 00:12:54.041
A wire transfer needing a date picker where an internal transfer does not: that is kind

182
00:12:54.041 --> 00:12:58.730
so a second reviewed template, and that is the right answer rather than a failure.

183
00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:05.478
Whichever of forty disclosure blocks apply to this product: that looks like the worst case and it is actually how many

184
00:13:05.478 --> 00:13:08.508
one disclosure template against a bound list of text.

185
00:13:08.508 --> 00:13:09.423
And the last one

186
00:13:09.423 --> 00:13:13.769
the screen should adapt to whatever the customer asks for: that is unbounded

187
00:13:13.769 --> 00:13:15.142
it is not a data problem

188
00:13:15.142 --> 00:13:20.631
and the correct response is to push back on the requirement rather than to reach for generation.

189
00:13:20.631 --> 00:13:22.575
Seven requirements. Two templates.

190
00:13:22.575 --> 00:13:29.037
That ratio is typical once you start classifying properly, and it is the number worth walking into the room with.

191
00:13:29.037 --> 00:13:31.750
Which leaves the question your reviewers will actually ask.

192
00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:33.529
Six practices make this hold up.

193
00:13:33.529 --> 00:13:39.034
Keep templates in version control, so a screen change is a pull request rather than a database row.

194
00:13:39.034 --> 00:13:43.371
Give each one an identifier and let the agent name only identifiers that exist

195
00:13:43.371 --> 00:13:48.153
rejecting anything unknown at the edge instead of falling back to something generated.

196
00:13:48.153 --> 00:13:56.103
Validate the data model against a schema before you send it, because a good template plus bad data renders a wrong screen rather than an error.

197
00:13:56.103 --> 00:14:02.220
Snapshot test each template against a fixed dataset, the way the conformance suites in the A2UI repository do.

198
00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:04.777
Log the template identifier with every surface

199
00:14:04.777 --> 00:14:07.613
so when someone asks what the customer actually saw

200
00:14:07.613 --> 00:14:11.839
the answer is a commit hash plus a data model and you can replay it exactly.

201
00:14:11.839 --> 00:14:14.229
And review the set rather than each screen.

202
00:14:14.229 --> 00:14:17.788
That last one is the whole argument: once a template is approved

203
00:14:17.788 --> 00:14:20.401
every dataset it can render is approved with it

204
00:14:20.401 --> 00:14:24.627
which is precisely why this satisfies a constraint that generated UI cannot.

205
00:14:24.627 --> 00:14:30.910
And one practical note on the last point: review the template alongside a representative dataset, not on its own.

206
00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:34.524
A reviewer looking at a component tree cannot picture forty rows.

207
00:14:34.524 --> 00:14:39.584
A reviewer looking at the template plus the empty case, the one case and the many case can.

208
00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:41.133
So, to put it together.

209
00:14:41.133 --> 00:14:47.302
Non-deterministic data is not the same problem as non-deterministic structure, and the two get conflated constantly.

210
00:14:47.302 --> 00:14:49.589
Cardinality, depth and content are all data

211
00:14:49.589 --> 00:14:55.013
and the template form of children handles all three from a component tree you wrote and reviewed once.

212
00:14:55.013 --> 00:14:59.533
Reach for a second template when the kind of thing changes, not when the amount does.

213
00:14:59.533 --> 00:15:02.777
Your brokerage payload is already four template bindings deep

214
00:15:02.777 --> 00:15:06.766
so the pattern is proven in your own codebase; what is missing is naming it

215
00:15:06.766 --> 00:15:09.212
writing the test for how many versus what kind

216
00:15:09.212 --> 00:15:16.444
and putting the governance around the template set so your reviewers can approve a catalogue instead of approving screens one at a time.

217
00:15:16.444 --> 00:15:21.656
That is a smaller piece of work than building a generation pipeline you would then have to defend.

218
00:15:21.656 --> 00:15:24.421
If you want a concrete next step, it is a short one.

219
00:15:24.421 --> 00:15:31.494
Take the brokerage payload you already have, and write down which of its four template bindings are how many and which are what kind.

220
00:15:31.494 --> 00:15:36.653
Then take the last ten screen requirements that came through, and classify each one the same way.

221
00:15:36.653 --> 00:15:42.396
My expectation is that eight of them are cardinality, one is degree, and one genuinely needs a new template.

222
00:15:42.396 --> 00:15:49.681
Put that number in front of whoever owns the constraint, because the argument you want to make is not that templates can be made to cope.

223
00:15:49.681 --> 00:15:54.361
It is that the variation they are worried about was never structural in the first place.
